-<article>
-<header class="jh-blog-post-header">
-<p class="blog-date jh-blog-post-date">2026-04-14</p>
-<h1 class="jh-blog-post-title">How AI Is Actually Used in Healthcare</h1>
-<p class="jh-blog-post-intro">AI in healthcare gets reported in two registers: miraculous breakthrough and impending catastrophe. Neither is accurate. The reality is more specific, more conditional, and more interesting than either headline.</p>
-<p class="jh-blog-post-byline">By <a href="/bio/">Jonathan Harris</a> &mdash; AI author and host of <a href="/podcast/">Turing’s Torch AI Weekly</a></p>
-</header>
-<h2>What AI in healthcare actually does</h2><p>The commercial applications are narrower than the press suggests. Medical imaging analysis — detecting tumours, fractures, and retinal disease in scans — is the most mature and evidenced. Transcription and clinical documentation automation is now widely deployed and genuinely saves clinician time. Triage and early-warning systems in acute care settings flag deteriorating patients earlier than manual observation. Operational planning tools help with bed allocation, scheduling, and supply chain. Drug discovery platforms are accelerating the hypothesis-generation phase of pharmaceutical research.</p>
-<h2>Where the evidence is actually solid</h2><p>Imaging AI has the strongest evidence base because the task is well-defined, the training data is abundant, and performance can be benchmarked against radiologist reads. Some systems now match or exceed radiologist accuracy on specific narrow tasks — detecting diabetic retinopathy in fundus photographs is the most frequently cited example. The clinical workflow integration is harder than the model performance: deploying a system that works in a research hospital is not the same as deploying one that works in an NHS district general on legacy PACS infrastructure.</p>
-<h2>Where it reliably struggles</h2><p>Anything that requires contextual clinical reasoning across a full patient history. Anything where training data under-represented certain patient groups and the model therefore performs worse for them. Anything where the deployment environment differs materially from the training environment. Anything where governance, liability, and regulatory approval have not been navigated. The gap between what the benchmark says and what the model does in the real ward is the persistent problem.</p>
-<h2>The governance and workflow problem</h2><p>A technically impressive model can fail in practice because it arrives at the wrong point in the clinical workflow, produces alerts that clinicians quickly learn to ignore, or creates documentation that does not fit the existing systems. Implementation is not a technical afterthought. It is most of the actual work. The organisations that have had success with clinical AI are the ones that treated workflow design and change management as primary, not secondary, concerns.</p>
-<section class="jh-blog-post-callout">
-<h2 class="jh-blog-post-callout-title">Questions answered in this article</h2>
-<div class="faq"><details><summary>Will AI replace doctors?</summary><p>No. The evidence from every serious implementation is that clinical AI augments medical judgement rather than replacing it. The value is in decision support, documentation reduction, and pattern detection at scale — not autonomous diagnosis.</p></details>
-<details><summary>Is AI in healthcare regulated?</summary><p>Yes. In the UK, clinical AI products require MHRA registration as software medical devices. In the US, the FDA has a pathway for AI/ML-based software as a medical device. The EU MDR applies across Europe. Regulatory approval is not optional for anything used in clinical decision-making.</p></details>
-<details><summary>What is the biggest barrier to AI adoption in the NHS?</summary><p>Infrastructure and interoperability. Many trusts run on legacy PACS, EPR, and pathology systems that pre-date modern data standards. Connecting a new AI system to reliable, standardised data is often harder and more expensive than building the AI.</p></details></div>
-</section>
-</article>
